From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cts: a sink's cts_latency_offset property moves its balanced
 latency
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The latency balancer gives every macro sink its clock pin's
max_clock_tree_path (rise and fall averaged) and pads the faster trees
until each macro's pin arrival plus that insertion delay meets the
register tree's average. There was no way to ask for a macro to be
clocked earlier or later than that on purpose: a parent over hardened
blocks whose crossings want block-granular useful skew, or whose blocks
drift early between CTS's placement parasitics and global route, could
only edit the block's abstract, which pre-CTS (ideal clock) timing also
reads.

The balancer now subtracts a sink instance's "cts_latency_offset" double
property (seconds) from the insertion delay it balances with, in both
places it adds one, through one helper. A positive offset lands the
block's internal clock that much later, a negative one earlier; CTS-0139
names each sink it applies to.

A macro tree whose only child is the register tree is a leaf to the
balancer: it pads the two trees' average arrivals against each other and
never looks at one macro. Where a sink of such a tree has the property,
the tree is no longer taken as a leaf, so the graph descends to each
macro, as it already does for a macro tree with clock-gated children.

Nothing else reads the property: STA, the H-tree's macro clustering and
-useful_skew's targets are unchanged, and a design without the property
balances as before.

cts::set_latency_offset <instance> <offset> sets it from Tcl, the offset
in the user time unit, before clock_tree_synthesis; a flow that calls it
on an OpenROAD without this change stops on an unknown command instead
of building an unskewed tree.

src/cts/test/latency_offset asks for one macro 50 ps late on
insertion_delay.def and checks that it lands within 15 ps of that after
the registers' mean arrival (54.5 ps here); asked for 0 it lands at 3.7
and the check fails.

Not upstreamed. Carried in bazel-orfs; retires on the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD with a per-sink latency target in CTS, or when nothing sets the
property.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
